// A recipe, written from md-docs/recipes/pdf-report.md by scripts/docsite/recipes.py;
// edit the recipe, not this file.
page RecipePdfReport(path: "/recipes/pdf-report", title: "Make a PDF report — Recipes", description: "Build a project into a PDF report: a page wrapped in Document, with a running footer, a chart drawn from data and a table that breaks across pages.") {
    RecipeShell("Make a PDF report", summary: "A project can build to a PDF instead of a site: the same pages, laid out on paper by the compiler, with no browser involved. This report shows a quarter's sales as a chart, a table and a total, with a page number in every footer:", outputs: ["pdf"], since: "", recipes: RECIPE_INDEX, sections: [Anchor(id: "how-it-works", label: "How it works"), Anchor(id: "variations", label: "Variations")], related: [RelatedLink(kind: "Recipe", title: "Render a template with data", to: "/recipes/render-a-template"), RelatedLink(kind: "Recipe", title: "Write a page as a Markdown file", to: "/recipes/markdown-pages"), RelatedLink(kind: "Recipe", title: "Render Markdown", to: "/recipes/render-markdown")], source: "md-docs/recipes/pdf-report.md", how: true, hasVariations: true, project: { "src/pages/PdfReport.wf": #"const MONTHS = [
    { month: "Jan", web: 41, store: 22 },
    { month: "Feb", web: 48, store: 25 },
    { month: "Mar", web: 55, store: 24 },
]

page Report(path: "/", title: "Q1 report", description: "Sales for the first quarter.") {
    Document(size: "A4", title: "Q1 report", author: "Acme Inc.") {
        Header(on: .rest) { Text("Acme Inc. · Q1 report").muted.sm.right }
        Footer { Text("Page {page} of {pages}").muted.sm.center }

        Heading("Q1 report").h1
        Text("Web sales grew every month; the shop held steady.")

        Heading("Sales by month").h2
        Chart(kind: .bar, data: MONTHS, x: "month", y: ["web", "store"], unit: "k", legend: true)

        Table(caption: "Sales by month, in thousands") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row { Table.Cell("Month")  Table.Cell("Web")  Table.Cell("Shop") }
            }
            Table.Body {
                for m in MONTHS {
                    Table.Row { Table.Cell(m.month)  Table.Cell("{m.web}k")  Table.Cell("{m.store}k") }
                }
            }
        }

        Card {
            style { break-inside: avoid }
            Text("Total: {MONTHS.map(m => m.web + m.store).sum()}k").bold
        }
    }
}
"#, "webfluent.app.json": #"{
  "name": "Q1 report",
  "build": {
    "output_type": "pdf",
    "pdf": {
      "page_size": "A4",
      "output_filename": "q1-report.pdf"
    }
  }
}
"# }) {
        code {
            Markdown("**Needs:** `\"output_type\": \"pdf\"` in `webfluent.app.json`, as below. `wf init q1 -t pdf` makes a project with it; replace its `src/pages/Report.wf` with the page.")
            CodeBlock(#"{
  "name": "Q1 report",
  "build": {
    "output_type": "pdf",
    "pdf": { "page_size": "A4", "output_filename": "q1-report.pdf" }
  }
}"#, title: "json")
            CodeBlock(#"const MONTHS = [
    { month: "Jan", web: 41, store: 22 },
    { month: "Feb", web: 48, store: 25 },
    { month: "Mar", web: 55, store: 24 },
]

page Report(path: "/", title: "Q1 report", description: "Sales for the first quarter.") {
    Document(size: "A4", title: "Q1 report", author: "Acme Inc.") {
        Header(on: .rest) { Text("Acme Inc. · Q1 report").muted.sm.right }
        Footer { Text("Page {page} of {pages}").muted.sm.center }

        Heading("Q1 report").h1
        Text("Web sales grew every month; the shop held steady.")

        Heading("Sales by month").h2
        Chart(kind: .bar, data: MONTHS, x: "month", y: ["web", "store"], unit: "k", legend: true)

        Table(caption: "Sales by month, in thousands") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row { Table.Cell("Month")  Table.Cell("Web")  Table.Cell("Shop") }
            }
            Table.Body {
                for m in MONTHS {
                    Table.Row { Table.Cell(m.month)  Table.Cell("{m.web}k")  Table.Cell("{m.store}k") }
                }
            }
        }

        Card {
            style { break-inside: avoid }
            Text("Total: {MONTHS.map(m => m.web + m.store).sum()}k").bold
        }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")
        }
        variations {
            Markdown("- **A cover page**: put the title in a `Background(on: .first) \{ … \}` with a gradient, then `PageBreak` before the content.\n- **A table of contents**: `TableOfContents(levels: 2)` lists the headings with the page each lands on, each a link.\n- **A draft mark**: `Watermark(\"DRAFT\")` draws large, faint text behind every page.\n- **One PDF per customer**: render a template with each customer's data instead of building the project ([Render a template with data](/recipes/render-a-template)).\n- **A slide deck**: `\"output_type\": \"slides\"` and a `Presentation \{ Slide \{ … \} \}` page make one PDF page per slide.\n\nSee [PDF and slides](/learn/17-pdf-and-slides) for everything a document can hold.")

        }
        Markdown("`wf build` writes `build/q1-report.pdf`: a page with the heading, the bar chart, the table and the total, and \"Page 1 of 1\" at the foot.\n\n- `\"output_type\": \"pdf\"` makes `wf build` write one PDF instead of a site. Every page of the project is laid out in turn; a report is usually one.\n- `Document` is the root of the paper: its size, and the file's title and author, which a PDF reader shows in the document's properties.\n- `Footer` is drawn in the bottom margin of every page, and inside it `page` and `pages` are the page's number and the count. `Header(on: .rest)` is drawn on every page but the first, which already has the title.\n- `Chart` draws the list of records as vector graphics: `x:` names the field along the bottom, `y:` the fields drawn as bars, one colour each.\n- A `Table` that runs past the foot of a page breaks between rows, and its header row is drawn again at the top of the next page.\n- `break-inside: avoid` keeps the total's `Card` on one page.\n- The data is a `const` here. A JSON file read at build time (`data months = \"months.json\"`) works the same way, so the report can be built again whenever the numbers change.\n\nThe look comes from the same CSS a web page gets: a theme, a `style \{ \}` block and the project's `.css` files style the PDF too.")

    }
}
